`@renown/sdk` is the core authentication SDK — credential verification, session management, cryptographic operations — and it is constructed through a builder:

```ts
const renown = await new RenownBuilder("my-app")
  .withBaseUrl("https://www.renown.id")
  .build();
```

Every dependency is injectable, which is what makes the SDK testable and re-hostable:

- `withBaseUrl(url)` — the Renown server, default `https://www.renown.id`;
- `withStorage(storage)` — user-data persistence, **default in-memory** (so a host that wants a session to survive a reload must supply one);
- `withEventEmitter(emitter)` — for state changes;
- `withCrypto(crypto)` — a pre-built crypto instance;
- `withKeyPairStorage(storage)` — key-pair storage, **from which crypto is built** if no instance is given;
- `withProfileFetcher(fetcher)` — a custom profile-enrichment strategy.

The division of labour with React is stated explicitly in `COMPONENTS.md`: **React components and hooks for Renown are provided by `@powerhousedao/reactor-browser`, not by `@renown/sdk` directly** — initialised with either a `<Renown appName onError>` component or the `useRenownInit` hook. So a Node or CLI consumer takes the SDK alone, and a React host takes reactor-browser, which wraps it.

